  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss. It is steeper along x, so plain gradient descent diverges along x once the learning rate is above 2/3. Runs start at x = 1.2, z = 1.6.",
    "Each step: new = old − learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with mean 0 and size 2.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) per axis, which is exactly 0 at b = 64. Seeded noise means Reset replays the same values.",
    "The orange arrow is the mini-batch step the ball takes next. The grey arrow is the full-data step, −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at 2× their true length. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as soon as the loss goes above 6 (diverged). Off-bowl positions are drawn at the bowl's edge, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The ball makes one update every 3 host ticks, about 10 updates per second at 1× speed. Each trail has the start point plus up to 40 landing points.",
    "Changing a setting during a run applies from the next step onward. New run, keep last path starts a fresh run with your current settings, uses the next noise values in the sequence, and keeps the last run as the ghost."
  ]
}